A||||3||GREENDANO||Urethane Bushing Question
Z||000000||GREENDANO||06-13-99||08:01 AM||||I'm in the middle of instaling urethane bushings in the front of my 85 GT.  I was wondering from you guys that have already done them one question.  Did the bushings in the lower control arm slide back out a bit after you tightened the bolt up.  They installed into the arm no problem, but when I tightened everything up, the bushings didn't stay seated against the shoulder of the metal sleeve of the cantrol arm.  I was wondering if anyone else has seen this problem?  Or isn't it a problem?  <br>  As far as I can tell it won't be a problem, but who knows for sure?<br>  Dan-O
Z||000001||theogre||06-13-99||03:00 PM||||without seeing it it's hard to say.....<p>Ideally you want them fully seated. if they aren't it could allow the arm to move front to back. not good. installing some brass washers might be a good idea. steel if that's all you can get, but make sure you coat them with antisieze compound or they will quickly rust and could cause squeeking.. if you can't find washers the right size, you'll have to make them. They don't have to be perfect, just take up the space. you want washers that have the same outside diameter as the bushing face and an inside diameter to fit over the inner sleeve of the bushing. use brass if you make them.
Z||000002||mwbackus||06-14-99||01:43 AM||||     I have heard of this before.  If I remember right, the person got them from the Fiero Store, but decided to grind some of the inner sleeve off so they would compress correctly rather than return them. I also recall some statements relating to a problem with these bushing applications on certain years.  <br>     Theogre is right on the money, you do not want this excess play in the control arms!  I would call the place of purchase and see what they have to say about it.
Z||000003||GREENDANO||06-15-99||05:56 PM||||Thanks for the input guys.  I ended up prying the control are towards the space and taking up the slack.  Then as I tightened the bolt the end swelled to hold it in place.  If the control arm wasn't held, it would slide back out.  I have about 100 miles on the new bushings and so far after inspecting them, they look good.<br>  Dan-O
